<section class="wrap section" id="data" aria-labelledby="data-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Data and method</p>
    <h2 id="data-title">Published baselines, with modelled detail and projections</h2>
    <p>Baselines are the APSC's published figures. Record-level detail, histories and projections are modelled from published open-source data, and every modelled figure is labelled.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Horizon data sources" tabindex="0">
    <table class="ruled">
      <caption>Data sources</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Used for</th><th scope="col">Type</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Australian Public Service Commission (APSC) APS Employment Data, 30 June 2025</td><td>Baseline headcount, separation rate, job family shares, agencies and locations</td><td><span class="tag tag--published">Published</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tier 1 open data: APS employment snapshots, Census, annual-report workforce tables and budget staffing estimates</td><td>Agency views before any system is connected</td><td><span class="tag tag--published">Published</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tier 2: validated agency file extracts</td><td>Deeper, agency-specific detail</td><td><span class="tag tag--agency">Agency data</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tier 3: optional live HR system connectors, priced per connector</td><td>Ongoing agency detail</td><td><span class="tag tag--agency">Agency data</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jobs and Skills Australia, education completions, skilled migration and the ACS Digital Pulse</td><td>National labour market and supply</td><td><span class="tag tag--published">Published</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hansard, Senate estimates, committee hearings and news</td><td>The daily intelligence feed</td><td><span class="tag tag--published">Published</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>How net impact is modelled</h3>
  <ol class="method">
    <li><strong>Task exposure.</strong> The share of task-hours exposed to generative AI in each job family (modelled, 27% to 46% across seven job families), anchored to Eloundou et al. (2023).</li>
    <li><strong>Augment or automate.</strong> Exposed hours are split 65% augmentation and 35% automation, anchored to Jobs and Skills Australia's Gen AI Capacity Study (2025).</li>
    <li><strong>Adoption reality.</strong> Only 60% of the theoretical effect is credited by FY2030, ramping over three years, anchored to the whole-of-government Copilot trial (Digital Transformation Agency, 2024).</li>
    <li><strong>Transition costs.</strong> New oversight and data roles, a reskilling drag of about 0.4 FTE-years for about 20% of affected staff, and the tooling, enablement, security and program management the capacity depends on.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="formula">Net impact = automation capacity released + augmentation headroom − new roles − annualised reskilling drag</p>
  <p class="table-note">All figures are full-time-equivalent capacity, not positions. The evidence supports reshaping through redeployment and mobility, not headcount cuts. Coefficients are editable assumptions in the platform.</p>

